      "text": "First, we need to determine the total number of blue and green tiles in the original three-row figure.\n\nThe figure has three rows. Each row consists of hexagonal tiles arranged in a specific pattern. The first row has 13 blue tiles, the second row has 6 green tiles, and the third row is empty as per the instructions. Therefore, the total number of tiles in the figure is:\n\n\\[ 13 + 6 = 19 \\]\n\nNext, we consider the new figure formed by adding a border of green tiles to the original figure. This border is a strip of green tiles that extends along the perimeter of the original figure. The border will add green tiles to the sides of the original figure.\n\nTo visualize the addition of the border, let's start with the original figure and add the boundary:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n & & & 13 \\text{ (blue tiles)} \\\\\n\\hline\n & & 6 \\text{ (green tiles)} & \\\\\n\\hline\n & & & \\\\\n\\hline\n & & & \\\\\n\\hline\n13 \\text{ (blue tiles)} & 6 \\text{ (green tiles)} & 13 \\text{ (blue tiles)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFrom the figure, we can see that the original figure contributed 2 rows of 13 blue tiles each totaling \\(2 \\times 13 = 26\\) blue tiles. The border alone consists of 6 green tiles (since the border is a strip of green tiles on three sides, \\(3 \\times 2 = 6\\)). Therefore, the new figure will have:\n\n- The original 19 tiles.\n- 6 additional green tiles from the border.\n\nThus, the total number of green tiles in the new figure is:\n\n\\[ 6 + 6 = 12 \\]\n\nTo find the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure, we calculate:\n\n\\[ 12 - 26 = -14 \\]\n\nThe difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-14}\n\\]",
