      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to determine the total number of tiles in the original figure and then add the border tiles. The original figure consists of 13 blue and 6 green tiles, so the total number of tiles is:\n\n\\[ 13 + 6 = 19 \\]\n\nNext, we need to consider the border of green tiles that is added around the original figure. The border will consist of green tiles all surrounding the original figure. Since the figure is a combination of hexagons, a natural way to think about the border is that it will form a larger hexagon around the original figure, with each side of the larger hexagon being composed of 2 original tiles plus some additional tiles from the boundary.\n\nLet's visualize the new figure. The original figure can be seen as one large hexagon with some additional tiles forming the border. The border will include all sides of the original figure plus an additional layer around the outside. Each side of the original figure (which is 3 tiles long) will be extended to 4 tiles by adding a 1-tile border around each side.\n\nSo, each side of the original figure will contribute an extra 1 tile, and there are 19 tiles, forming 6 sides. Therefore, the total number of border tiles is:\n\n\\[ 19 + 5 = 24 \\]\nThe border is 4 tiles long on each side, plus 1 additional tile on each side, totaling 5 tiles.\n\nNow, we need to find the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure. The original figure has 6 green tiles, and the border adds an additional 4 green tiles on each side, contributing a total of 5 green tiles in the border. So, the total number of green tiles in the new figure is:\n\n\\[ 6 + 4 + 1 = 11 \\text{ (original green tiles)} + 5 \\text{ (border green tiles)} = 16 \\text{ green tiles} \\]\n\nThe total number of blue tiles remains 13. Therefore, the difference between the number of green tiles and the number of blue tiles in the new figure is:\n\n\\[ 16 - 13 = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
